The '''Cundill History Prize''' is an annual Canadian [[literary award]] for [[history]] writing in English.<ref name="about">{{cite web |title=About |url=https://www.cundillprize.com/about |website=Cundill Prize |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> It was established in 2008 by [[Peter Cundill]] and is administered by [[McGill University]].<ref name="2008establish1">{{cite news |last=Adams |first=James |date=April 17, 2008 |title=Canadian establishes world's largest historical non-fiction prize |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/canadian-establishes-worlds-largest-historical-non-fiction-prize/article20383809/ |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref><ref name="2008establish2">{{cite news |date=April 18, 2008 |title=Former Montrealer sets up $75,000 prize for history writing |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/former-montrealer-sets-up-75-000-prize-for-history-writing-1.697409 |work=[[CBC News]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> The prize is awarded to an author whose book, published in the past year, demonstrates "historical scholarship, originality, literary quality and broad appeal". No restrictions are set on the topic of the book or the nationality of the author, and English translations are permitted.<ref name="about" /><ref name="submit">{{cite web |title=Submit |url=https://www.cundillprize.com/submit |website=Cundill Prize |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240420233912/https://www.cundillprize.com/submit |archive-date=April 20, 2024 |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref>

At a value of {{US$|75000}}, the grand prize is the [[List of richest literary awards|largest prize in the world for a book of non-fiction in English]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Wheeler |first=Brad |date=December 1, 2022 |title=Tiya Miles' slavery story ''All That She Carried'' wins the Cundill History Prize |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/books/article-all-that-she-carried-tiya-miles-cundill-history-prize/ |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Wood |first=Heloise |date=November 9, 2023 |title=Guardian's Branigan scoops Cundill History Prize for 'extraordinary' debut |url=https://www.thebookseller.com/news/guardians-branigan-scoops-cundill-history-prize-for-extraordinary-debut |work=[[The Bookseller]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> In addition, two finalists are awarded "Recognition of Excellence" prizes of $10,000 each. For translated works, 80% of the prize goes to the author, and 20% goes to the translator.<ref name="submit" /> The recipients of the prize are selected by a jury of historians and writers chosen by McGill.<ref>{{cite web |title=Finding the Next Cundill History Prize Winners |url=https://www.cundillprize.com/triage |website=Cundill Prize |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> The prize has been called "the closest approximation to a [[Nobel Prize]] for history".<ref>{{cite journal |last=Duggan |first=Lawrence G. |date=March 2023 |title=Review of ''Contesting Orthodoxies in the History of Christianity: Essays in Honour of Diarmaid MacCulloch. Studies in Modern British Religious History'', by E. Gebarowski-Shafer, A. Null, & A. Ryrie |journal=[[Anglican and Episcopal History]] |volume=92 |issue=1 |pages=116–118 [116] |jstor=27223460 |issn=0896-8039}}</ref>

== History ==
The '''Cundill International Prize in History''' was announced on April 17, 2008, at [[McGill University]] by [[Peter Cundill]], a London-based investment manager and graduate of McGill. A grand prize of {{US$|75000}}, as well as two "Recognition of Excellence" prizes of $10,000, would be awarded once a year to authors whose books were "determined to have a profound literary, social and academic impact on the subject". Books were required to be published in English or French.<ref name="2008establish1" /><ref name="2008establish2" /> Cundill said that he "was surprised to learn there were no major prizes in history" and added that "I'm an investment researcher of finance and I think there's an analogy between the two disciplines – both study the past to understand the present and predict the future."<ref>{{cite web |date=April 17, 2008 |title=Largest literary prize in history makes history |url=https://www.mcgill.ca/channels/news/largest-literary-prize-history-makes-history-100163 |website=Channels – McGill University |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> The inaugural prize in November 2008 was administered by the Dean of the Faculty of Arts at McGill, along with the [[McGill Institute for the Study of Canada]] (MISC).<ref>{{cite web |date=November 25, 2008 |title=McGill announces winner of inaugural Cundill International Prize in History |url=https://www.mcgill.ca/newsroom/channels/news/mcgill-announces-winner-inaugural-cundill-international-prize-history-102975 |website=Newsroom – McGill University |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref>

In 2010, the prize was renamed the '''Cundill Prize in History'''.<ref>{{cite news |last=Agrell |first=Siri |date=November 14, 2010 |title=British church historian wins McGill's Cundill Prize |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/news/national/british-church-historian-wins-mcgills-cundill-prize/article1314184/ |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> Cundill died on January 24, 2011,<ref>{{cite news |last=Fine |first=Philip |date=February 17, 2011 |title=Peter Cundill found wealth where others feared to tread |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/report-on-business/peter-cundill-found-wealth-where-others-feared-to-tread/article567099/ |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> and the 2011 prize was limited to books that were published in English.<ref>{{cite news |date=November 15, 2011 |title=Italian historian wins $75K Cundill Prize |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/entertainment/italian-historian-wins-75k-cundill-prize-1.1050998 |work=[[CBC News]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> The prize was retitled again in 2013 as the '''Cundill Prize in Historical Literature''',<ref>{{cite news |last=Robertson |first=Becky |date=November 21, 2013 |title=Anne Applebaum wins Cundill Prize in Historical Literature |url=https://quillandquire.com/awards/2013/11/21/anne-applebaum-wins-cundill-prize-in-historical-literature/ |work=[[Quill & Quire]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> and the partnership with MISC continued up to 2016.<ref>{{cite web |date=November 18, 2016 |title=''The Work of the Dead'' wins Cundill Prize in Historical Literature |url=https://www.mcgill.ca/newsroom/channels/news/work-dead-wins-cundill-prize-historical-literature-264219 |website=Newsroom – McGill University |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref> McGill relaunched the prize for its 10th year in 2017: it was renamed the '''Cundill History Prize''', and the prize's logo and website design were overhauled.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pymm |first=Francesca |date=May 19, 2017 |title=$75k Cundill History Prize relaunched |url=https://www.thebookseller.com/news/75k-cundill-history-prize-relaunched-555041 |work=[[The Bookseller]] |access-date=November 19, 2025}}</ref>

